---
import CheckStatus from '../components/CheckStatus.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  CHECK_STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS,
  CHECK_STATUSES,
  DIFFICULTY_DESCRIPTIONS,
  DIFFICULTY_LABELS,
  DIFFICULTY_LEVELS,
  EVIDENCE_DESCRIPTIONS,
  EVIDENCE_LABELS,
  EVIDENCE_LEVELS,
  FORMAT_DESCRIPTIONS,
  FORMAT_LABELS,
  FORMATS,
  PATTERN_STRENGTH_DESCRIPTIONS,
  PATTERN_STRENGTH_LABELS,
  PATTERN_STRENGTH_LEVELS,
  SENTIMENT_LABELS,
  SENTIMENT_LEVELS,
  SITE,
} from '../config/site';
import ContactEmail from '../components/ContactEmail.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy" description={`How ${SITE.name} sources, reviews, and labels its reporting.`}>
  <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>

  <h2>How AI is used in producing this publication</h2>
  <p>
    AI may help discover sources and prepare first drafts. It does not decide what gets published. A human editor
    checks and approves every article before it goes live, and nothing generated or imported by AI publishes
    automatically — every change reaches the live site only through a reviewed, human-approved pull request merged
    to <code>main</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because of that, articles here carry a house byline ("{SITE.defaultAuthor}") instead of a person's name. It
    would be misleading to put an individual editor's name on AI-drafted copy as though they wrote it themselves.
    The "Reviewed by" credit on each article is the real, accountable human who checked it before it went live.
  </p>

  <h2>Content types</h2>
  <p>We publish {FORMATS.length} kinds of articles:</p>
  <dl>
    {
      FORMATS.map((format) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{FORMAT_LABELS[format]}</dt>
          <dd>{FORMAT_DESCRIPTIONS[format]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>How we source a Workflow</h2>
  <p>
    A researched Workflow is never a rewording of one person's specific published process, however well credited.
    It's a synthesis across multiple independent sources covering the same or closely related technique, written in
    our own words, going beyond any single source by calling out where approaches disagree and adding depth from
    documented tool knowledge those sources didn't individually cover. When a technique genuinely traces back to one
    creator's write-up, we credit them and link to their original for the full step-by-step rather than reproducing
    it here, so our coverage doesn't take the traffic a full rewrite would.
  </p>
  <p>
    An idea Workflow is different again: it's an automation or combination we've assembled ourselves from tools'
    real, documented capabilities, not lifted from any existing tutorial because nobody's published that exact
    combination. We say so plainly near the top of the piece, source every individual capability it depends on, and
    never claim it's been hands-on verified end to end.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence labels</h2>
  <p>Every article carries one of three evidence labels, shown on its card and at the top of the article:</p>
  <dl>
    {
      EVIDENCE_LEVELS.map((level) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{EVIDENCE_LABELS[level]}</dt>
          <dd>{EVIDENCE_DESCRIPTIONS[level]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
  <p>
    Which levels actually apply depends on format. A Pulse is always <strong>Researched</strong>, enforced by the
    schema, since it synthesizes public discussion rather than reporting one sourced change. A Brief is{' '}
    <strong>Announced</strong> or <strong>Researched</strong>. A Workflow is, as a matter of current practice,
    always <strong>Researched</strong> too, we haven't yet published one we hands-on tested ourselves, though the
    schema allows it once we do. <strong>Tested</strong> is reserved for a genuine hands-on Test article.
  </p>

  <h2>Effort to try</h2>
  <p>
    Every article's sidebar carries an effort rating, except a Pulse, which has nothing to "try" and shows an
    overall-mood reading instead, see below. It's about time investment, not skill, a tool you already know well
    might be "Quick" for you even if we rated it "Moderate" for a typical reader coming to it cold:
  </p>
  <dl>
    {
      DIFFICULTY_LEVELS.map((level) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{DIFFICULTY_LABELS[level]}</dt>
          <dd>{DIFFICULTY_DESCRIPTIONS[level]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Pulse: aggregate sentiment, not individual quotes</h2>
  <p>
    A Pulse reports what designers, as a group, are actually saying, gathered from spaces like Reddit, Dribbble and
    Behance comments, and Designer News, deliberately not from engineer-audience forums like Hacker News, which
    would skew the read toward tech-industry opinion rather than design-practitioner opinion. It never names or
    quotes anyone individually, even when a claim traces back to one specific comment, the body reads as a synthesis
    of a pattern, not a transcript of who said what. We only call something a pattern once we've found more than one
    independent source saying something similar, every one of them linked in the article's sources for a reader to
    verify, even though none are named in the prose.
  </p>
  <p>
    In place of an effort rating and the "before you use it" checklist below, a Pulse's sidebar shows two readings
    of its own. The overall mood is one of{' '}
    {SENTIMENT_LEVELS.map((level, i) => (
      <>
        {i > 0 && (i === SENTIMENT_LEVELS.length - 1 ? ', or ' : ', ')}
        <strong>{SENTIMENT_LABELS[level]}</strong>
      </>
    ))}
    . How solid the pattern is gets its own reading too:
  </p>
  <dl>
    {
      PATTERN_STRENGTH_LEVELS.map((level) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{PATTERN_STRENGTH_LABELS[level]}</dt>
          <dd>{PATTERN_STRENGTH_DESCRIPTIONS[level]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 id="before-you-use-it">Before you use it</h2>
  <p>
    Every article's sidebar except a Pulse's includes a "Before you use it" checklist covering client data handling,
    usage rights, disclosure obligations, accessibility, and cost, alongside the tools it discusses and (for Test
    and Workflow articles) a quick summary. Each item is marked with one of three labels, so you can tell at a
    glance what we confirmed and what we didn't. We never guess to fill a gap.
  </p>
  <dl>
    {
      CHECK_STATUSES.map((status) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            <CheckStatus status={status} />
          </dt>
          <dd>{CHECK_STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS[status]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 id="media">Images, video, and animation</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are drafted as text. Any image, GIF, video, or animation is added by a human editor during review, and we
    only use media we made ourselves, have permission to use, or that its owner offers for embedding (like a
    YouTube video's own embed). Media from someone else is credited underneath it. Every image and animation has a
    text description for people using screen readers, and animations pause for readers who've asked their device
    to reduce motion. Videos don't load anything from YouTube until you press play.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and withdrawals</h2>
  <p>
    When new information changes our assessment, we update the article and bump its "updated" date; if we re-check
    the underlying facts without changing the conclusion, we bump "last verified" instead. When something we
    reported turns out to be wrong, no longer applies, or is withdrawn by its maker, we withdraw the article: its
    URL keeps working, but the page shows a short withdrawal notice explaining why instead of the original body. A
    withdrawn article is removed from listings, search, and our RSS feed, and marked so search engines don't index
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spotted an error, a claim that's out of date, or a source that doesn't say what we said it does? Email{' '}
    <ContactEmail /> with the article and what's wrong, and we'll check it against the sources.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
